Offline pre-tokenizers: one model's own tokenizer.json pre_tokenizer per file.

WHY THESE EXIST
  The gates in tok/ compare what tok.ParsePreTokenizer builds at runtime against
  what scripts/gentok baked into tok/pretokenizers.go. Both used to need the
  network to say anything about a REAL model, so the alternative was a
  hand-written approximation of a tokenizer.json -- which tests the test author's
  memory of the artefact, not the artefact. These are the artefacts, dumped by
  the generator itself:

      go run ./scripts/gentok -dump tok/testdata/pre tok/pretokenizers.go

WHAT IS IN A FILE
  The pre_tokenizer object VERBATIM, byte for byte as the repo serves it, under a
  one-line {"pre_tokenizer": ...} wrapper. The wrapper is there because
  ParsePreTokenizer takes a tokenizer.json and looks for that key -- a bare object
  would be testdata the parser it is testdata FOR cannot read. Nothing inside the
  braces is reformatted, so a diff against the upstream file stays readable.

  The rest of tokenizer.json is NOT here. It is megabytes of vocabulary and merges
  that the GGUF already carries; WithTokenizer overrides the pre-tokenizer only.

WHY THESE ELEVEN
  tok/pipeline.go's header names SEVEN distinct pipeline shapes that occur in the
  wild. One file per shape, plus four that share a shape with one already here,
  three of them differing INSIDE it -- which is the exact difference a shape-only sample set
  misses, and each was found by a model tokenizing wrong rather than by design.

  llama-bpe shares qwen2's shape and differs in the digit quantifier. gpt-4o
  shares it too and differs in the whole splitting ALGORITHM: o200k splits letter
  runs by case and hangs the contraction off the end of one, where llama3 takes a
  single \p{L}+ run and treats the contraction as its own leading alternative.
  The table gave gpt-4o llama3's parameters, and every gpt-oss model tokenized
  "Isn't" as three tokens instead of two until 2026-09-19. Ten names in the
  directory carry that shape.

      file             shape                                              repo @ main
      ---------------  -------------------------------------------------  -----------
      qwen2.json       Split -> ByteLevel(use_regex=false)                 Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B @ 97b0c614
      gpt-2.json       ByteLevel(use_regex=true)                           evilfreelancer/ruGPT3XL @ d35fdf92
      smollm.json      Digits(individual) -> ByteLevel(use_regex=true)     HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM-135M @ 1d461723
      falcon3.json     Punctuation -> ByteLevel(=true) -> Digits(indiv)    tiiuae/Falcon3-7B-Base @ bf3d7ed5
      falcon-h1.json   Punctuation -> Split -> ByteLevel(=false) -> Split  tiiuae/Falcon-H1-34B-Base @ db4eb5f3
      llama-bpe.json   Split(digit group 3) -> ByteLevel(use_regex=false)  NousResearch/Meta-Llama-3-8B @ 315b2009
      gpt-4o.json      Split(o200k case runs)  -> ByteLevel(use_regex=false) Xenova/gpt-4o
      seed-coder.json  Split(no newline tail)  -> ByteLevel(use_regex=false) ByteDance-Seed/Seed-Coder-8B-Base

  seed-coder is the eighth for the same reason as gpt-4o: it shares llama3's
  shape and differs inside it. Its punctuation run is ` ?[^\s\p{L}\p{N}\r\n]+`
  with no [\r\n]* tail, so "):\n" is two pieces where llama3 makes one; the
  table gave it llama3's parameters until the Seed-OSS fixture's tokenizer.json
  golden caught it.
      hunyuan-dense.json  DigitGroups -> KanaHanRuns -> HunyuanSplit -> ByteLevel(=false)
                                                                           tencent/Hunyuan-0.5B-Instruct

      deepseek-v3.json    the same shape and the same three patterns   deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V3

  deepseek-v3.json is the re-deriving command below (it reformats). Its three
  Splits are hunyuan-dense's byte for byte -- Hunyuan's tokenizer is DeepSeek
  V3's -- and its table row, with hy_v4's and joyai-llm's (whose repos serve the
  identical object), still read them as three llama3 splits after
  hunyuan-dense's was corrected, so "12345" tokenized digit by digit until
  DeepSeek V3.2's fixture compared against tokenizer.json.

      spark2_5.json       DigitGroups -> KanaHanRuns -> SparkSplit -> Digits(indiv) -> ByteLevel(=false)
                                                                           XHToken/Spark-X2.5-1.7B

  spark2_5.json is the seventh shape. Its first two Splits are Hunyuan's; its
  third is a variant (no [\r\n]* tail on the punctuation run, and [\r\n] a
  piece of one character where Hunyuan's has \s*[\r\n]+), and a Digits stage
  follows. Its table row read all three as llama3 splits until the variant
  became pretok.OpSparkSplit; tok.TestSparkSplitsAsItsTokenizerDoes holds it
  to HuggingFace tokenizers on the shared corpus.

  hunyuan-dense.json and spark2_5.json are written by
  scripts/hunyuansplitgold.py, which REFORMATS
  (json.dump) where the generator copies bytes; the pattern strings are the
  artefact's. Its three Splits are each a whole pattern rather than the
  alternation skeleton, and reading them as the skeleton gave three llama3
  splits that isolated every digit.

  Each is https://huggingface.co/<repo>/resolve/main/tokenizer.json, fetched
  2026-09-12. The name on the left is the tokenizer.ggml.pre name; the repo on the
  right is the one llama.cpp's convert_hf_to_gguf_update.py names as DEFINING that
  name, resolved at llama.cpp@3057bb66.

  llama-bpe is the one indirection: its defining repo meta-llama/Meta-Llama-3-8B
  is gated, so the file comes from the mirror in scripts/gentok/pipeline.go --
  admitted there only because both repos serve tokenizer.json at git blob
  b197f72effb9d5ed16ee0f5663e11e4cfac2ba62, i.e. the same bytes rather than a
  lookalike.

RE-DERIVING ONE
  curl -sL https://huggingface.co/<repo>/resolve/main/tokenizer.json \
    | python3 -c 'import sys,json; print(json.dumps({"pre_tokenizer": json.load(sys.stdin)["pre_tokenizer"]}))'

  That reformats. The generator does not -- prefer the -dump command above, and
  expect a diff if the repo has moved since the date named here. A file that no
  longer matches its row in tok/pretokenizers.go is a FINDING, not a stale fixture:
  either the model changed its tokenizer or the table is wrong about it.
